Here's a compact React example that uses useRef to read an element's actual width/height, computes a zoomScale = Math.min(desiredWidth/actualWidth, desiredHeight/actualHeight), and applies it via CSS transform. It uses ResizeObserver (with a window.resize fallback) to update when the element size changes.

```jsx
import React, { useRef, useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function ZoomToFit({ desiredWidth, desiredHeight, children }) {
  const elRef = useRef(null);
  const [scale, setScale] = useState(1);

  useEffect(() => {
    const el = elRef.current;
    if (!el) return;

    const update = () => {
      const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();
      const actualWidth = rect.width || el.offsetWidth;
      const actualHeight = rect.height || el.offsetHeight;
      if (actualWidth && actualHeight) {
        const zoomScale = Math.min(desiredWidth / actualWidth, desiredHeight / actualHeight);
        setScale(zoomScale);
      }
    };

    // initial measurement
    update();

    // observe size changes
    let ro;
    if (typeof ResizeObserver !== 'undefined') {
      ro = new ResizeObserver(() => update());
      ro.observe(el);
    } else {
      window.addEventListener('resize', update);
    }

    return () => {
      if (ro) ro.disconnect();
      else window.removeEventListener('resize', update);
    };
  }, [desiredWidth, desiredHeight]);

  // Container constrains visible area; inner element is scaled from top-left
  return (
    <div style={{ width: desiredWidth, height: desiredHeight, overflow: 'hidden' }}>
      <div
        ref={elRef}
        style={{
          transform: `scale(${scale})`,
          transformOrigin: 'top left',
          display: 'inline-block' // keep element's intrinsic size for measurement
        }}
      >
        {children}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

// Usage example:
// <ZoomToFit desiredWidth={800} desiredHeight={600}>
//   <div style={{ width: 1000, height: 900 }}>Large content to scale down</div>
// </ZoomToFit>
```

Notes:
- getBoundingClientRect() gives sub-pixel precision; offsetWidth/offsetHeight are fallbacks.
- transformOrigin: 'top left' keeps scaling predictable. If you need the element to occupy less layout space when scaled, wrap it and adjust container sizes accordingly.
- ResizeObserver is preferred for element-level size changes; the code falls back to window resize when unavailable.

