Here's a simple, robust pattern to map entities with circular references to DTOs in C# without any libraries: use a cache (Dictionary) keyed by the source object (or its ID) to record DTOs as soon as you create them, then fill their reference properties. That avoids infinite recursion and preserves shared references.

Example: Entity graph with Parent/Children circular references -> EntityDto with same shape.

```csharp
public class Entity
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public Entity Parent { get; set; }
    public List<Entity> Children { get; } = new List<Entity>();
}

public class EntityDto
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public EntityDto Parent { get; set; }
    public List<EntityDto> Children { get; } = new List<EntityDto>();
}

public class EntityMapper
{
    // Cache maps source Entity -> created EntityDto
    private readonly Dictionary<Entity, EntityDto> _cache = new Dictionary<Entity, EntityDto>();

    public EntityDto Map(Entity source)
    {
        if (source == null) return null;

        // If already mapped, return cached DTO (prevents cycles)
        if (_cache.TryGetValue(source, out var existing))
            return existing;

        // Create shallow DTO and register it before mapping refs
        var dto = new EntityDto
        {
            Id = source.Id,
            Name = source.Name
        };
        _cache[source] = dto; // crucial: register early

        // Now populate references (safe because of cache)
        dto.Parent = Map(source.Parent);
        foreach (var child in source.Children)
            dto.Children.Add(Map(child));

        return dto;
    }

    public List<EntityDto> MapAll(IEnumerable<Entity> sources)
    {
        _cache.Clear();
        var list = new List<EntityDto>();
        foreach (var s in sources)
            list.Add(Map(s));
        return list;
    }
}

// Usage example
var root = new Entity { Id = 1, Name = "Root" };
var child = new Entity { Id = 2, Name = "Child", Parent = root };
root.Children.Add(child);
// Circular: child.Parent -> root, root.Children -> child

var mapper = new EntityMapper();
var dtoRoot = mapper.Map(root);
```

Notes and alternatives:
- The above Dictionary<Entity, EntityDto> uses reference equality for keys (which is what you want). If your entities have unique IDs and you prefer value-based keys, use Dictionary<int, EntityDto> and key by Id.
- If your DTOs are intended for JSON serialization, circular object graphs may cause serializer errors. Options: a) omit back-references (e.g., ParentId instead of Parent), b) use a serializer that supports reference handling, or c) flatten relationships for transport.
- You can generalize this pattern (generic mapper, reflection, or expression-based mapping) but the cache-and-register-early technique is the essential part to handle cycles correctly.

