Here are several alternatives in C# 5.0 to check whether a variable is in an array, with short examples and notes on when to use each.

1) Enumerable.Contains (LINQ)
using System.Linq;
string[] options = { "Option 1", "Option 2", "Option 3" };
string variable = "Option 2";
bool isIn = options.Contains(variable);

- Simple and readable. Requires using System.Linq.

2) Array.IndexOf
bool isIn = Array.IndexOf(options, variable) >= 0;

- No LINQ needed; works fine for small arrays.

3) Array.Exists
bool isIn = Array.Exists(options, o => o == variable);

- Similar to your original example; explicit predicate.

4) List<T>.Contains
var list = new List<string>(options);
bool isIn = list.Contains(variable);

- Use when you already have a List or need list operations.

5) HashSet<T>.Contains (best for many repeated lookups)
var set = new HashSet<string>(options);
bool isIn = set.Contains(variable);

- Fast O(1) lookup for repeated checks; you can pass a comparer for case-insensitive lookups: new HashSet<string>(options, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase).

6) Case-insensitive check with LINQ
using System.Linq;
bool isIn = options.Any(o => string.Equals(o, variable, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));

7) switch (for small fixed sets)
switch (variable)
{
    case "Option 1":
    case "Option 2":
    case "Option 3":
        // found
        break;
    default:
        // not found
        break;
}

Performance guidance: for one-off checks on small arrays, Contains/IndexOf/Array.Exists are fine. For many lookups, build a HashSet for much faster checks. For case-insensitive matching, use StringComparer or StringComparison as shown.

